 - **2026-10-09 (M14, #577 follow-up):** A window larger than the GPU's texture limit no longer closes PdfCraft. Vendored egui-wgpu 0.36.2 fits the window's surface within `max_texture_dimension_2d` and draws egui at the matching scale, on every path that sizes a surface (start-up restore, resize, maximise, DPI change, a recreated surface); upstream is emilk/egui#8361. Root cause of the Outlook-only trigger: winit creates the window on the launching app's monitor and converts the restored size at that monitor's scale. Unit tests cover the 8600 × 3423 case and edge sizes; a Windows Sandbox (WARP) run of a window one pixel over the limit panicked before and kept drawing after. Estimates unchanged.
 - **2026-10-09 (M14, #519):** A GPU that can't configure the window's surface at launch no longer panics, so PdfCraft's OpenGL retry runs. Vendored egui-wgpu configures a new window's surface inside wgpu error scopes (`winit::catch_errors`) and returns a failure as an error, so eframe returns `Error::Wgpu` and the existing OpenGL retry (#530) runs (a device lost during that configure still panics, on the first frame). Before, wgpu's default handler panicked: on a 2015 Intel GPU with no D3D12 driver wgpu picked the GL adapter over WARP and timed out in `Surface::configure` (`GpuWaitTimeout`). A real-device regression test (a wgpu validation error inside `catch_errors`) panicked with the scopes removed; in Windows Sandbox a forced configure failure panicked unpatched and retried with OpenGL patched (Sandbox has no OpenGL 2.0, so the OpenGL window itself was not shown there). Defaulting Windows to DX12 only (WARP instead of wgpu's GL backend) is left to the maintainers. Overall estimate unchanged (about 30–35%).
